"""English — details, sira_c_2 (50 questions)."""

D = {
"sira_201": "Every standard biography opens with this lineage: Muhammad ﷺ, son of 'Abdullah, son of 'Abd al-Muttalib, of Banu Hashim within Quraysh, the tribe that kept the Ka'bah. His father died before his birth; after his mother's death his grandfather 'Abd al-Muttalib cared for him, then his uncle Abu Talib. The Qur'an recalls it: \"Did He not find you an orphan and give you shelter?\" (93:6). A Muslim learns that the last Prophet ﷺ began life as an orphan in the care of his family.",
"sira_202": "The verse is in Surah As-Saff, where 'Isa, peace be upon him, tells his people that he confirms the Torah and brings \"good tidings of a messenger to come after me, whose name is Ahmad\" (61:6). Ahmad and Muhammad share the Arabic root hamd, praise. The verse adds that when that messenger came with clear proofs, they called it plain magic. A Muslim learns from 61:6 that the Prophet ﷺ was announced by the prophet who came before him.",
"sira_203": "The Qur'an explains why it was not sent down at once: \"a Qur'an which We have divided, that you may recite it to the people over a prolonged period\" (17:106). To those who asked why it did not come in one piece, the answer was: \"Thus, that We may strengthen your heart thereby\" (25:32). The first words came in the cave of Hira, \"Read in the name of your Lord\" (96:1). A Muslim learns that the Qur'an met the community's needs as they arose, over a whole lifetime.",
"sira_204": "Every standard biography records that Abu Bakr accepted the call without hesitation, and that through him came several early companions, among them 'Uthman, az-Zubayr, 'Abd ar-Rahman ibn 'Awf, Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas and Talha. He spent his wealth freeing Muslims tortured for their faith, Bilal among them. The Qur'an names him as the companion in the cave: \"when he said to his companion: Do not grieve; indeed Allah is with us\" (9:40). A Muslim learns that the first to believe was also the first to give.",
"sira_205": "The standard biographies record how 'Ali came to that household: in a year of hardship in Makkah, the Prophet ﷺ took his young cousin from the house of Abu Talib to ease his uncle's burden, as Abu Talib had once taken in the orphaned Muhammad ﷺ. So 'Ali saw the first revelation from inside the home and believed as a boy. On the night of the Hijra he slept in the Prophet's ﷺ bed to deceive Quraysh. A Muslim learns that faith is not measured by age.",
"sira_206": "Every standard biography tells how Zayd, captured as a boy and sold, came to Khadija and then to the Prophet ﷺ; when his father and uncle came to buy him back, Zayd chose to stay, and the Prophet ﷺ freed him and declared him his son before the Ka'bah. He was called Zayd ibn Muhammad until the Qur'an ordered: \"Call them by the names of their fathers\" (33:5). He is the only companion named in the Qur'an (33:37). A Muslim learns from Zayd's choice what the Prophet's ﷺ company was worth.",
"sira_207": "The verse comes in Surah Al-Ahzab, after the account of Zayd's marriage (33:37): \"Muhammad is not the father of any of your men, but he is the Messenger of Allah and the seal of the prophets; and Allah is ever, of all things, Knowing\" (33:40). Earlier the surah had said, \"He has not made your adopted sons your sons\" (33:4), and ordered, \"Call them by the names of their fathers\" (33:5). A Muslim reads two truths here: an adopted child keeps his father's name, and no prophet comes after Muhammad ﷺ.",
"sira_208": "Surah Al-Jumu'ah takes its name from this day: \"When the call is made for the prayer on the day of Jumu'ah, hasten to the remembrance of Allah and leave trade\" (62:9). The next verse releases them: \"And when the prayer has ended, disperse in the land and seek from the bounty of Allah\" (62:10). Jumu'ah means gathering, and the Prophet ﷺ led it in his mosque, addressing the people from the minbar first. A Muslim learns that once a week the whole community sets its business aside to pray together.",
"sira_209": "Every standard biography records that when the Prophet ﷺ was too ill to come out, he told his household to have Abu Bakr lead the prayer; 'A'isha asked him to choose another, saying her father was soft-hearted and would weep, and he repeated the order. Once, supported by two men, he came out and sat beside Abu Bakr while he prayed. After his death the companions recalled that he had put Abu Bakr forward in their religion, and put him forward in their affairs. A Muslim learns that the Prophet ﷺ prepared his community for his absence.",
"sira_210": "The standard biographies record that 'Umar was stabbed in the mosque of Madinah as he stood to lead fajr, by a slave named Abu Lu'lu'a, and had another man complete the prayer. Before he died he named a council of six companions to choose the next caliph, and asked 'A'isha's permission to be buried beside the Prophet ﷺ and Abu Bakr, which she gave. His caliphate had lasted about ten years, in which the Muslims opened Syria, Iraq, Persia and Egypt. A Muslim learns that 'Umar died as he had lived, in prayer.",
"sira_211": "The standard biographies record that Abu Bakr was chosen on the day the Prophet ﷺ died, in the gathering of the Ansar and Muhajirun at the hall of Banu Sa'ida. In his first address he told the people to obey him as long as he obeyed Allah and His Messenger ﷺ, and to correct him if he went wrong. In those two years he sent out the army of Usama, fought the tribes that broke away, and had the Qur'an collected into one volume. A Muslim learns how much a short time can hold.",
"sira_212": "Every standard biography records Abu Bakr's answer to those who wanted to let the zakat go: he swore he would fight anyone who separated the prayer from the zakat, since the Qur'an joins the two, even over a single young goat once paid to the Messenger ﷺ. 'Umar first thought him harsh, then saw that his heart had been opened to the truth. The armies also faced Musaylima, who claimed prophethood; many reciters fell at Yamama. A Muslim learns that the community held to the whole religion, not part of it.",
"sira_213": "The standard biographies record how the title arose. Abu Bakr had been called khalifat rasul Allah, the successor of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; 'Umar would then be the successor of the successor of the Messenger, a name that would grow longer with each caliph. The companions addressed him instead as amir al-mu'minin, and it stayed. The Qur'an calls the believers al-mu'minun throughout, and speaks of \"those in authority among you\" (4:59). A Muslim learns that the leader's title named him by the people he served, not by a crown.",
"sira_214": "The Qur'an names the mosque in the verse of the Night Journey: \"Glory be to Him who took His servant by night from the Sacred Mosque to al-Aqsa Mosque, whose surroundings We have blessed\" (17:1). The believers faced it in Makkah and in the first months in Madinah, until revelation changed the qibla: \"So turn your face toward the Sacred Mosque\" (2:144), after foretelling that the foolish would ask what turned them from their qibla (2:142). A Muslim learns that the direction of prayer is set by Allah's command.",
"sira_215": "Every standard collection of the Prophet's ﷺ words records that he said saddles are not to be fastened for a journey to any mosque except three: the Sacred Mosque, his own mosque, and al-Aqsa Mosque. The Qur'an names the first two: the Sacred Mosque as the qibla, \"turn your face toward the Sacred Mosque\" (2:144), and al-Aqsa as the goal of the Night Journey (17:1). The third he built with his own hands on arriving in Madinah. A Muslim learns that these three places hold a rank no other mosque shares.",
"sira_216": "The standard biographies record that on the day of 'Arafah the Prophet ﷺ addressed the people from his camel in the valley of 'Uranah, and that on that same day and plain the verse came down: \"This day I have perfected for you your religion and completed My favour upon you and have approved for you Islam as religion\" (5:3). At the end he asked the crowd whether he had conveyed the message; they answered yes, and he asked Allah to bear witness. A Muslim learns that the religion was declared complete at 'Arafat.",
"sira_217": "Every standard biography records how he framed it: he asked the crowd which day, which month and which city it was, and when they answered that all three were sacred, he told them that their blood, property and honour were as inviolable to one another as that day, month and city. He also abolished the blood claims and usury of the days of ignorance, beginning with his own family. The Qur'an had said: \"Whoever kills a soul, it is as if he had killed all mankind\" (5:32). A Muslim learns that another Muslim's life, wealth and dignity are sacred.",
"sira_218": "Every standard biography records his words at 'Arafah: he told the crowd he had left among them that which, if they held fast to it, would keep them from ever going astray, the Book of Allah. He then asked what they would say when questioned about him, and they answered that he had conveyed the message and fulfilled his trust. The Qur'an had said: \"Hold firmly to the rope of Allah all together\" (3:103). A Muslim learns that the Prophet's ﷺ inheritance was not gold or land but the Book.",
"sira_219": "The Qur'an sets the pilgrimage in its season: \"Hajj is in the well-known months\" (2:197), and speaks of \"the day of the greater pilgrimage\" (9:3). Dhul-Hijjah is one of the four sacred months: \"of them, four are sacred\" (9:36). The farewell pilgrimage was the only Hajj the Prophet ﷺ performed after the Hijra; he stood at 'Arafah, spent the night at Muzdalifah, threw the pebbles at Mina, and told the people to take their rites from him. A Muslim learns that every Hajj since follows that pattern.",
"sira_220": "The standard biographies record that the Prophet ﷺ died in the room of 'A'isha and that his family washed him without removing his garment, 'Ali washing while al-'Abbas, his sons al-Fadl and Qutham, and Usama ibn Zayd helped. The people then came in group after group to pray over him without an imam: the men, then the women, then the children. Abu Bakr told them that a prophet is buried where he dies, so his grave was dug in that room. A Muslim learns that the last service to the Prophet ﷺ was done within his household.",
"sira_221": "The standard biographies record that Rabi' al-Awwal was also the month in which the Prophet ﷺ had reached Madinah at the Hijra, so his arrival in the city and his death in it fell in the same month, ten years apart. He died on a Monday in the room of 'A'isha, resting against her, after a fever of several days; his last words asked for the highest companion. Abu Bakr kissed his forehead and said that Allah would not join two deaths upon him. A Muslim learns that the Prophet ﷺ was a mortal servant.",
"sira_222": "The verse had come down after Uhud, when a rumour spread that the Prophet ﷺ was killed: \"Muhammad is not but a messenger; messengers have passed on before him. So if he was to die or be killed, would you turn back on your heels?\" (3:144). Every standard biography records that when 'Umar denied the death, Abu Bakr said that whoever worshipped Muhammad should know that Muhammad had died, and whoever worshipped Allah should know that Allah lives and does not die, then recited 3:144, and the people wept. A Muslim learns that faith rests on Allah, not on any man.",
"sira_223": "The standard biographies record that Abu Hurayra came to Madinah in the year of Khaybar and lived among the poor of the Suffa, the covered part of the mosque, so as to stay close to the Prophet ﷺ while others were busy in markets and fields. He told how he once complained of forgetting what he heard, and the Prophet ﷺ had him spread out his cloak and gather it to his chest, after which he forgot nothing. A Muslim learns that much of the Prophet's ﷺ words reached the world through one man who chose knowledge over wealth.",
"sira_224": "The standard biographies record that the Prophet ﷺ once put his arm around the boy and prayed that Allah would give him understanding of the religion and teach him the interpretation of the Book. Ibn 'Abbas, nephew of the Prophet's ﷺ wife Maymunah, stayed in his house as a child and was still young when he died. Under 'Umar he sat with the elders of Badr, and when 'Umar asked them about Surah An-Nasr, only Ibn 'Abbas saw that it announced the Prophet's ﷺ approaching death. A Muslim learns that understanding of the Qur'an is Allah's gift.",
"sira_225": "The standard biographies record the instruction the Prophet ﷺ gave Mu'adh as he sent him to Yemen: he was going to a people of the Book, so he should call them first to the testimony of faith, then to the five daily prayers, then to the zakat taken from their rich and given to their poor; and he should beware of the prayer of the wronged, for no veil stands between it and Allah. Mu'adh was still in Yemen when the Prophet ﷺ died. A Muslim learns the order in which the religion is taught.",
"sira_226": "The Qur'an names the two hills: \"Indeed, as-Safa and al-Marwa are among the symbols of Allah; so whoever makes Hajj to the House or performs 'Umrah, there is no blame upon him for walking between them\" (2:158). Ibrahim, peace be upon him, had settled Hajar and Isma'il there, \"in a valley without cultivation, near Your sacred House\" (14:37). Every standard account records Hajar running between the hills in search of water for her child until Zamzam sprang up. A Muslim who walks between them walks in the footsteps of a mother's search.",
"sira_227": "Every standard biography records that the Aws and the Khazraj had fought for years, and that their last great battle, the day of Bu'ath, came shortly before the Hijra and left both sides weakened. Men of the Khazraj met the Prophet ﷺ at the pilgrimage season, and the next year the two tribes pledged to him together at 'Aqabah. The Qur'an then reminded them: \"when you were enemies and He brought your hearts together\" (3:103). A Muslim learns that Islam turned the enemies of yesterday into one people, the Ansar.",
"sira_228": "The verse belongs to Surah Al 'Imran, revealed in Madinah, and opens with a command: \"And hold firmly to the rope of Allah all together and do not become divided. And remember the favour of Allah upon you: when you were enemies and He brought your hearts together, and you became, by His favour, brothers; and you were on the edge of a pit of the Fire, and He saved you from it\" (3:103). The next verse asks for a community calling to good (3:104). A Muslim learns that unity between believers is a favour to be guarded.",
"sira_229": "The verse belongs to Surah Al-Hashr, revealed after the expulsion of Banu an-Nadir: \"And those who settled in the home and in faith before them love those who emigrated to them, and find in their breasts no need for what they were given, and give preference over themselves, even though they are in privation\" (59:9). The verse before praises the poor emigrants who left their homes (59:8), and the verse after those who come later and pray for both (59:10). A Muslim learns that giving what one needs oneself is the measure of generosity.",
"sira_230": "The Qur'an sends the believers to the Prophet's ﷺ example in plain words: \"There has certainly been for you in the Messenger of Allah an excellent example\" (33:21), and: \"Whatever the Messenger gives you, take; and what he forbids you, refrain from\" (59:7). It joins obedience to him with obedience to Allah: \"He who obeys the Messenger has obeyed Allah\" (4:80). His companions watched how he prayed, fasted, ate and judged, and passed it on. A Muslim learns that the Qur'an is followed through the way the Prophet ﷺ lived it.",
"sira_231": "The name comes from the Qur'an, which ranks the generations in one verse: \"And the first forerunners among the Muhajirun and the Ansar and those who followed them with good conduct, Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with Him\" (9:100). Those who followed, at-tabi'un, learned the Qur'an and the Sunnah from the companions themselves in Madinah, Makkah, Kufa, Basra and Damascus, and through them the companions' reports reached the hadith collections. A Muslim learns that the religion passed from the Prophet ﷺ onward, hand to hand.",
"sira_232": "The word isnad comes from sanad, a support: the chain is what the text leans on. A report in Sahih al-Bukhari or Sahih Muslim is written chain first, the collector naming his teacher, who names his, back to the companion who heard the Prophet ﷺ or saw him act; only then comes the matn. Each narrator had to name the person he received the report from. The Qur'an demands the same care: \"if there comes to you a disobedient one with information, investigate\" (49:6). A Muslim learns that a saying reaches the Prophet ﷺ only through named witnesses.",
"sira_233": "Sahih means sound: each compiler, Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Bukhari and his student Muslim ibn al-Hajjaj, gathered only reports whose chains he had examined and accepted. Both arranged their books by subject, from faith and purification through prayer, zakat, fasting and pilgrimage to the life of the Prophet ﷺ, each report written with its full chain of narrators. A hadith found in both is called muttafaq 'alayh, agreed upon, and holds the highest rank among reports. A Muslim learns where to look first to know what the Prophet ﷺ said.",
"sira_234": "Every standard biography records that the Prophet ﷺ let his camel choose where it knelt when he entered the city, and that his mosque was built there, with the rooms of his wives against its wall. He lived beside that mosque for ten years, led the prayers in it, and died in 'A'isha's room, where he was buried; Abu Bakr and 'Umar were later laid beside him. The Qur'an calls the city al-Madinah: \"It was not for the people of Madinah to remain behind the Messenger of Allah\" (9:120). A Muslim who visits prays in his mosque and greets him at his grave.",
"sira_235": "The Qur'an calls the city simply al-Madinah, the city, as in \"the hypocrites of Madinah\" (9:101); the epithet al-Munawwarah, the illuminated, was added by the Muslims once it became the city of the Prophet ﷺ, lit by his presence and the revelation. Its older name appears once, in the mouths of those who wanted to desert at the Trench: \"O people of Yathrib, there is no place for you here, so return\" (33:13). Makkah's epithet al-Mukarramah, the honoured, points to the Ka'bah. A Muslim learns that the two cities are named for what Allah placed in them.",
"sira_236": "The verse belongs to Surah Al-An'am, revealed in Makkah: \"And this is a Book which We have sent down, blessed and confirming what was before it, that you may warn the mother of cities and those around it\" (6:92). The same expression returns in Surah Ash-Shura: \"that you may warn the mother of cities and those around it\" (42:7). Makkah is the mother of cities because it holds the first house built for worship: \"the first House established for mankind was that at Bakkah\" (3:96). A Muslim learns that the warning began at the centre and spread outward.",
"sira_237": "Every standard biography records that Khadija bint Khuwaylid hired the young Muhammad ﷺ, already known in Makkah as al-Amin, the trustworthy, to take her goods to Syria, and that her servant Maysara returned with an account of his honesty; she then proposed marriage. When the first revelation came at Hira and he returned trembling, she wrapped him in a cloak and said that Allah would never disgrace him, because he kept the ties of kinship, bore other people's burdens and gave to the poor. A Muslim learns that the first believer was a working woman who had judged his character first.",
"sira_238": "The standard biographies record that all the Prophet's ﷺ children except Ibrahim were Khadija's: al-Qasim and 'Abdullah, who died as infants, and his daughters Zaynab, Ruqayyah, Umm Kulthum and Fatimah. She died in Makkah before the Hijra, in the same year as Abu Talib, a year the biographies call the year of sorrow. Years later 'A'isha said she was never as jealous of any wife as of Khadija, whom she had never seen, because the Prophet ﷺ spoke of her so often. A Muslim learns that the Prophet ﷺ never forgot his first wife.",
"sira_239": "Every standard biography records that Abu Talib took his nephew in after 'Abd al-Muttalib died, took him on his trade journey to Syria, and refused when Quraysh demanded that he hand him over; he shared the boycott in the ravine of Banu Hashim. At his deathbed the Prophet ﷺ urged him to say that there is no god but Allah, while Abu Jahl pressed him to keep the religion of 'Abd al-Muttalib, and he died without saying it; then came the verse: \"you do not guide whom you like, but Allah guides whom He wills\" (28:56). A Muslim learns that guidance belongs to Allah alone.",
"sira_240": "The standard biographies record that al-'Abbas came to Badr on the side of Quraysh, was taken prisoner, and ransomed himself and others; to the captives of Badr the Qur'an said: \"O Prophet, say to whoever is in your hands of the captives: If Allah knows any good in your hearts, He will give you better than what was taken from you\" (8:70). He had stood beside his nephew at the pledge of 'Aqabah, and at Hunayn, when the Muslims scattered, he held the Prophet's ﷺ mule and called the men back. A Muslim learns that the uncle once on the other side ended as a pillar at the Prophet's ﷺ side.",
"sira_241": "The Qur'an calls the battle \"the day of the criterion, the day when the two armies met\" (8:41), because it separated truth from falsehood. Every standard biography records that the Muslims there were a little over three hundred, facing an army about three times their number, and had set out expecting a caravan, not a battle. The Qur'an speaks of the angels sent to them: \"I will reinforce you with a thousand from the angels\" (8:9). When Hatib ibn Abi Balta'a later erred, the Prophet ﷺ reminded 'Umar that he had been at Badr. A Muslim learns why Badr remained the highest honour among the companions.",
"sira_242": "The verse comes in Surah Al-Anfal, the surah of the spoils, revealed after Badr: \"And know that anything you obtain of spoils, then indeed for Allah is one fifth of it and for the Messenger and for the near relatives and the orphans and the needy and the traveller, if you have believed in Allah and in what We sent down to Our servant on the day of the criterion\" (8:41). The surah had opened with the fighters' question: \"They ask you about the spoils\" (8:1). A Muslim learns that even the gains of war carry a share for the weak.",
"sira_243": "The verse belongs to Surah Al-Hujurat, which teaches the believers how to treat one another: \"O mankind, indeed We have created you from male and female and made you peoples and tribes that you may know one another. Indeed, the most noble of you in the sight of Allah is the most righteous of you\" (49:13). Two verses earlier the surah forbade one people to mock another (49:11). Every standard biography records that in the farewell sermon the Prophet ﷺ said that no Arab has merit over a non-Arab except by righteousness. A Muslim learns that tribe and colour are for recognition, not rank.",
"sira_244": "Every standard biography records that the second pledge took place at night, in secret, at the pass of 'Aqabah near Mina during the pilgrimage season; the delegation slipped away from their sleeping companions to meet the Prophet ﷺ, who came with his uncle al-'Abbas. Two women were among them, Nusayba bint Ka'b and Asma' bint 'Amr. The people of Yathrib pledged to protect him as they protected their own women and children, and he answered that his blood was their blood. A Muslim learns that Madinah opened its doors to Islam through a promise made in the dark.",
"sira_245": "Every standard biography records that the Prophet ﷺ asked the delegation to bring forward twelve chiefs, nine from the Khazraj and three from the Aws, and told them they would be guarantors over their people as the disciples had been for 'Isa, son of Maryam, while he was the guarantor for his own people. Among them were As'ad ibn Zurara, al-Bara' ibn Ma'rur and Sa'd ibn 'Ubada. The number recalls the chiefs of the Children of Israel: \"and We appointed among them twelve leaders\" (5:12). A Muslim learns that from its first night the community had men answerable for it.",
"sira_246": "The Qur'an records both names. The old one appears once, in the mouths of those who wanted to abandon the Prophet ﷺ at the battle of the Trench: \"O people of Yathrib, there is no place for you here, so return\" (33:13). The new one appears wherever the Qur'an speaks of the believers' city: \"the people of Madinah and those around them\" (9:120). The Prophet ﷺ also called it Taybah, the good, and every standard biography records that he asked Allah to make it beloved to the Muslims. A Muslim learns that the city was renamed by the man it sheltered.",
"sira_247": "Every standard biography records what moved 'Uthman: Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman, returning from the campaigns in Armenia and Azerbaijan, had heard soldiers from Syria and Iraq dispute over their recitations, and asked the caliph to act before the community differed over its Book. 'Uthman sent for the sheets that Abu Bakr had collected and that Hafsa, the Prophet's ﷺ widow, kept, had Zayd ibn Thabit and three men of Quraysh copy them, and sent a copy to each province. A Muslim learns that the single text recited everywhere goes back to those copies.",
"sira_248": "The Qur'an speaks of its own language many times: \"Indeed, We have sent it down as an Arabic Qur'an that you might understand\" (12:2); the trustworthy Spirit brought it down \"in a clear Arabic tongue\" (26:195). It also gives the rule behind this: \"And We did not send any messenger except in the language of his people\" (14:4). Quraysh, masters of that language, were challenged to bring one surah like it (2:23). A Muslim recites the Qur'an in Arabic wherever he lives, and reads a translation to understand its meaning.",
"sira_249": "Every standard biography records what happened when the wealth from Bahrain arrived: the Ansar came in numbers to the fajr prayer, and the Prophet ﷺ smiled and said that he knew they had heard of Abu 'Ubayda's return. He told them to rejoice and hope for good, then said that it was not poverty he feared for them, but that the world would be spread out before them as it had been for those before, and that they would compete for it and it would destroy them as it had destroyed those. A Muslim learns that the day of plenty is the day to guard the heart.",
"sira_250": "Every standard biography records that 'Umar, faced with dated documents that named no year, consulted the companions on where the Muslim years should begin, and that they chose the year of the Hijra over the year of the Prophet's ﷺ birth or of the first revelation, because the Hijra founded the community. The year begins with Muharram, the first of the twelve months the Qur'an names: \"the number of months with Allah is twelve\" (9:36). The Hijra itself is recalled in 9:40. A Muslim counts the years from the day the community found its home.",
}
